  "query_preview": "..\nMostly cloudy skies today with isolated to scattered light mist\nand showers moving through eastern Pima county into \nsoutheastern Pinal county, and across Cochise county. Light \nmist and showers will continue to pop up throughout the day as \nresidual moisture remains in the area from the weekend. \nSatellite derived PWATs are now between 0.75\" to 1\" across the \nsouthern Arizona. The upper level pattern continues to be \ndominated by a strong trough across the western half of the US \nwith the center of low pressure located in eastern Oregon. \nPressure gradients are still tight enough to lead to breezy \nsouth to southwest winds today sustained at 15 to 25 mph gusting\nto 35 mph. \n\nAs the trough moves eastward the low pressure center will dip \ninto the Great Basin with eventually a cut off low moving off \nthe main trough into southern Arizona. At the same time, high \npressure will back into the southwest US, this will tighten \npressure gradients again leading to strong east winds as early \nas Thursday night. Current NBM probabilities have a large area \nfrom Tucson eastward between 45-80% for greater than 50 mph \nwind gusts with some areas have a 30-50% for greater than 60 mph\nwind gusts. Current models suggest the peak intensity of winds \nwill be Friday afternoon into the overnight hours with elevated \nwinds from Thursday afternoon through Saturday morning. \n\nThere continues to be a degree of uncertainty just how intense\nthis wind event will be. It will depend on how far the high \npressure moves westward and the alignment and intensity of the \ncut off low. Generally though, models point to a late work week\nto early weekend breezy east to southeast wind event focused \nfrom Tuscon eastward.",
